  "documentation" : {
    "decisions" : [ {
      "content" : "# 1. Record architecture decisions\n\nDate: 2025-10-17\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nWe need to record the architectural decisions made on this project.\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will use Architecture Decision Records, as [described by Michael Nygard](http://thinkrelevance.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions). See additional info [here](../adr-info.md).\n\n## Consequences\n\nSee Michael Nygard's article, linked above. For a lightweight ADR toolset, see Nat Pryce's [adr-tools](https://github.com/npryce/adr-tools).\n",
      "date" : "2025-10-17T00:00:00Z",
      "format" : "Markdown",
      "id" : "1",
      "status" : "Accepted",
      "title" : "Record architecture decisions"
    }, {
      "content" : "# 2. Use Excalidraw as a digital whiteboard\n\nDate: 2025-10-17\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nProject white boarding and stickies are not shareable or trackable over time\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will use [Excalidraw](https://excalidraw.com) for sharing diagrams and save relevant renderings\nto the doc/diagrams/*.excalidraw for documentation purposes over time.\n\nWhen collaborating with multiple devs the online multiuser feature of Excalidraw can be used but any changes should be exported and stored in the repo.\n\n## Consequences\n\nLeveraging Excalidraw and retaining the excalidraw output in the repo will allow for architecture/design/collaboration to be retained and update as the project progresses.\n",
      "date" : "2025-10-17T00:00:00Z",
      "format" : "Markdown",
      "id" : "2",
      "status" : "Accepted",
      "title" : "Use Excalidraw as a digital whiteboard"
    } ]
  },
